# Vertices 4 and 5 are identical, so edge 4 has length zero. The rest is the
# corner_corner bowtie. Note that the two edges flanking a zero-length edge
# share a point of 3-space -- the census collapses them, and any implementation
# must treat that as adjacency rather than as an unresolvable 3D intersection.
note = a zero-length edge, on top of a corner-corner degeneracy
knot = unknot
zero_length_edges    == 1
corner_corner        >= 4
coincident_vertices  >= 3
spatial              == 0

# KNOWN FAILURE (2026-08-13). LinkEmbedding2/3/4's class documentation lists
# "line segments that have length 0" among the degeneracies they handle, but a
# zero-length edge in a closed polygon necessarily makes its two neighbours
# share a point of 3-space, and ComputeEdgeEdgeIntersection rejects that as the
# unfixable "edges intersect in 3D" case (error code 6). This is not specific to
# this fixture: a bare planar square with one duplicated vertex fails the same
# way. The fix is to treat vertices joined by a zero-length edge as one point --
# what TakeCensus in embedding_fixtures.hpp does -- before the 3D-intersection
# test. Remove these markers once that lands.
# Scoped to 2,3,4: LinkEmbedding (the floating-point class) handles a
# zero-length edge without complaint, so this is a regression relative to it.
xfail_exact     = 2,3,4 | zero-length edge rejected as a 3D intersection
xfail_cross     = 2,3,4 | zero-length edge rejected as a 3D intersection
xfail_invariant = 2,3,4 | zero-length edge rejected as a 3D intersection
xfail_rotation  = 2,3,4 | zero-length edge rejected as a 3D intersection
xfail_symmetry  = 2,3,4 | zero-length edge rejected as a 3D intersection
